{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TDjD7ExuGisG45qB7gPHbU","type":"tool_result","content":"2:## *\"Four signals, and the question they exist to answer\"*\n9:## Attention Budget\n43:## 🧭 Soundings\n94:## Why This Chapter Matters\n116:## What You'll Learn\n131:## ⚪ §1 — What You Can Ask, and What You Already Know\n194:### Instrumentation is the precondition\n212:### What a probe is not\n228:## ⚪ §2 — Four Signals\n251:### Why baggage counts\n261:### One limit, stated early\n269:### The collector\n314:## 🔵 §3 — Numbers Over Time\n320:### A metric is a number with a shape\n328:### Labels, and the cost of one\n340:### The denominator\n354:### The boundary that gets tested\n380:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: What You Can Ask, and What the Numbers Report Against\n453:## 🔵 §4 — Pulling, Not Being Pushed\n459:### The arrow\n473:### The one exception, and how narrow it is\n529:### The pieces\n547:### Asking questions of what you collected\n559:### Where Prometheus fits, and where it does not\n579:## 🟡 §5 — Following One Request\n585:### The wall\n597:### Context, and moving it\n611:### Span and trace\n669:### Jaeger\n681:### Spans you didn't ask for\n701:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Pull, Push, and the Path One Request Took\n768:## 🔵 §6 — Lines From Everywhere\n776:### Why `kubectl logs` is not an archive\n780:**Rotation.** The kubelet rotates container log files. The defaults are `containerLogMaxSize` of 10Mi and `containerLogMaxFiles` of 5 — and critically, **only the contents of the latest log file are available through `kubectl logs`**. A chatty container blows through 10Mi in minutes, and the lines you wanted are in a rotated file the command will not read.\n782:**Restarts.** The kubelet keeps the logs of one terminated container so that `kubectl logs --previous` can reach one restart back. One. Not three, not \"since the Pod was created.\"\n784:**Eviction.** When a Pod is evicted, its containers are evicted with their logs. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §4 — pods that start and then don't stay]* The Pod that got OOMKilled and evicted at 3 a.m. took the evidence with it.\n792:### The three architectures\n840:**1. A node-level logging agent.** One agent per node, reading the container log files the runtime already writes, forwarding them to a backend. This is the default answer, and the reason is structural: the logs are already on the node's filesystem, for every container the node runs, whether or not the application cooperated. One agent covers everything.\n844:**2. A dedicated sidecar container** in the application Pod, collecting that application's logs. Useful when an application writes to a file inside the container rather than to stdout, or needs per-application processing. The cost is one extra container per Pod rather than one per node — which, on a node running forty Pods, is a meaningfully different resource bill.\n846:**3. The application pushes directly** to a logging backend from within the application. No collector at all; the app knows the backend's address and speaks its protocol. Simple, and it couples your application code to your logging vendor.\n850:### Fluentd and Fluent Bit\n864:| **Input** | Plugins gather information from sources — log files, OS metrics |\n877:## 🔵 §7 — Is the Service Doing What Users Expect\n887:### Three letters that get swapped\n911:### Error budgets, in one clause\n919:### The four golden signals\n972:### RED and USE\n990:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Lines, Signals, and the Question They Answer\n1010:**Rotation:** the kubelet rotates container logs — `containerLogMaxSize` 10Mi and `containerLogMaxFiles` 5 by default — and only the *latest* log file's contents are available through `kubectl logs`. Even within a single container run, older output can already be out of reach.\n1050:## ☀️ §8 — One Question, Four Instruments\n1095:### The other thing that kept happening\n1105:### Where that leaves you\n1117:## Exam Alert! 🚨\n1147:## Practice Questions\n1270:**Q14.** *[retrieval: ch6]* A logging agent must run on every node, including nodes added after deployment. Which workload resource is correct?\n1279:**Q15.** Which correctly describes the relationship between Fluentd and Fluent Bit?\n1288:**Q16.** A team commits: \"99.95% of search requests will return in under 200ms, measured monthly.\" No penalty is attached. This statement is:\n1297:**Q17.** Which are the four golden signals of monitoring?\n1306:### Answers and Explanations\n1374:**Q14 — C.** A DaemonSet's contract is one Pod per node, maintained as nodes join and leave. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §7 — one per node, and work that ends]* This is the canonical DaemonSet use case, and node-level log collection is the canonical example of it.\n1379:**Q15 — B.** Fluent Bit is \"an open source telemetry agent,\" created in 2014 \"as a lightweight log processor, developed by the Fluentd team at Treasure Data for constrained environments such as embedded Linux\"; it is \"a sub-project of Fluentd,\" and both \"are commonly deployed on Kubernetes as node-level logging agents (DaemonSets)\" [source: fluent-bit-overview-2026-08-23].\n1384:**Q16 — C.** A target value on a measurement, with no consequence attached. Apply the test: \"ask 'what happens if the SLOs aren't met?': if there is no explicit consequence, then you are almost certainly looking at an SLO\" [source: sre-book-service-level-objectives-2026-08-31].\n1389:**Q17 — B.** \"The four golden signals of monitoring are latency, traffic, errors, and saturation\" [source: sre-book-four-golden-signals-2026-08-23].\n1396:## Chapter Summary\n1426:## 🏆 Safe Harbor\n1436:## The Voyage Ahead"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95a83325-4699-403f-9a95-418f171c160c","uuid":"bc8ab687-299c-4c85-8b67-138395f3e2ad","timestamp":"2026-08-31T21:19:05.542Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"2:## *\"Four signals, and the question they exist to answer\"*\n9:## Attention Budget\n43:## 🧭 Sounding